WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.980
Canadian investors building AI exposures should watch Anthropics $190-$200 billion 2028 revenue forecast,

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.160
which carries a 67% chance of a $2 trillion valuation this year.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:19.180
That single probability is now a sizing tool, not a headline, and it changes how you build

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:24.480
the next unit of AI risk inside a tax-free savings account, or TFSA.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.520
This is Modern Investing Techniques, episode 140.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.420
Quick reminder, everything we discuss here is for education and entertainment.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.140
The practice investment of the day uses simulated trades with no real money.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.480
I'm not a licensed financial advisor, and this isn't financial advice.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.160
Always do your own research before putting real money to work.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:53.320
The S&amp;P 500 closed at 7,786, down two tenths of 1%.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:01.700
The NASDAQ composite finished at 26,729, down three tenths of 1%, and that is the benchmark

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.640
this show lives or dies by.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:12.940
The Toronto Stock Exchange, or TSX, composite slipped to 36,730, down one tenth of 1%,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.740
so the caution was broad rather than local.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.720
Stock futures back from the weekend are pointing to more of the same careful tone,

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.600
and US public debt sitting at $39.8 trillion is leaning on anything that needs lower yields

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:28.420
to look cheap.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.480
The Bank of Canada and the Federal Reserve remain the two anchors,

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.380
and markets are still pricing only limited cuts through year end,

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.920
which is why rate-sensitive sleeves deserve a second look before you add duration.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.780
AI infrastructure names kept the leadership tape,

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.440
while energy and materials lagged the session.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.740
A split that matters the moment you size a commodity name later today.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.220
Remember, about 14 days ago, we picked LNTH on the merger catalyst.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:04.900
It closed down 1.54%, a clean reminder that even an announced deal can stumble on integration risk.

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:08.460
Those atthropic numbers I opened with are not a curiosity.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:09.820
They are a worksheet.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:17.100
Valuation-driven AI exposure means you size a high-growth name from a disclosed multi-year revenue target

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.740
and a probability weighted valuation, not from yesterday's momentum candle.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:27.140
Anthropics $190-$200 billion 2028 revenue range,

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.980
paired with a 67% chance of a $2 trillion valuation this year,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.020
gives you a concrete benchmark against current private market pricing.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.920
Implantation is deliberately boring.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.300
Screen for companies that have actually published multi-year revenue targets,

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:50.760
then run a simple discounted cash flow, or DCF, against public comps in your brokerage platform or a spreadsheet.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.440
The method has historically earned its keep when growth stocks sold off on valuation nerves,

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:00.560
but the underlying demand for compute and infrastructure stayed intact.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.000
The pattern we saw in earlier AI build-out cycles.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.220
The risks are not abstract.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.800
Execution shortfalls can make those forecasts miss,

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:15.400
and private marks can stay disconnected from public multiples for longer than your patients will like.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.780
Pair every name that survives the screen with volume confirmation on the public proxies you can actually buy,

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.620
because a beautiful forecast with empty tape is just a story.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.480
Inside a TFSA you apply the same framework against TSX listed AI proxies,

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.820
such as the Horizons Global AI Exchange Traded Fund or ETF,

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:40.700
and I have no financial relationship with that issuer or any platform I mention.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:45.600
Treat the 67% figure as a live market implied odds ratio.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:52.140
Update it when new data arrives rather than locking a position size in January and hoping December agrees.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:59.000
That is how you stop chasing the last green candle and start underwriting a forecast you can defend at the dinner table.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.300
That same discipline, a catalyst, a number, and a defined loss,

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.500
is how we will treat today's simulated weekly hold.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.420
This is a simulated trade for learning purposes.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.300
No real money is involved, and this is not a recommendation to buy or sell anything.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.860
The pick is Quester Energy, tickerqec.to,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:23.940
listed on the TSX held Monday through Friday as the default weekly hold.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:30.260
The catalyst is Quebec Gas Policy Progress plus HCCO Technology Updates reported today,

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.300
a name-specific fundamental trigger rather than a blind bet on the commodity tape.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.420
Technically, the stock has been trading above its 50-day moving average on the daily chart,

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.100
with volume running near the 20-day average,

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.700
nearest support at the 20-day line and resistance at recent swing highs.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.820
Risk is policy reversal or a delayed technology milestone,

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:56.660
so the simulated stop sits 7% below that 20-day moving average

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.820
to cap the loss if the thesis breaks.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.840
The five-day target is plus 4% to plus 8% if the momentum holds.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:09.100
Confidence is medium, the policy catalyst and volume alignment support the setup,

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.600
but single-factor commodity exposure is the uncertainty.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:18.280
Last week's simulated TBBK hold taught a rule I am applying here in fresh form.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:22.640
Always cross-check sector rotation before you size a catalyst entry.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.680
Energy and materials lagged this session, so this is not a crowded leadership tape.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.540
That is exactly why confidence stays medium,

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.340
and the stop is written down before the first fill,

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.100
not after the first red day.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:40.440
What this teaches is the conversion of a sector-specific policy headline into an entry

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.760
with explicit risk parameters, so you can practice catalyst identification

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.260
without pretending you can forecast the gas price.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.680
We will track the simulated position all week and close the books on Friday.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.920
Watch whether QEC.TO holds that 20-day line,

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.980
and whether volume stays near its 20-day average.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.800
If either fails, the stop does the talking.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.440
Yesterday's simulated weekly hold was TBBK,

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:10.900
an earnings-driven entry built on record profitability and raised guidance in FinTech lending.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:16.400
We simulated buying at $68 US dollars and 93 cents on the Monday open,

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:21.040
and exiting at $69 US dollars and 14 cents on the Thursday close.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.040
A three calendar day window of market data.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:31.100
The position gained 3 tenths of 1% or $3.5 on a $1,000 simulated ticket.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:36.080
Over that same window, the NASDAQ moved down 3 tenths of 1%,

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.260
so the trade produced 6 tenths of 1% of alpha versus the benchmark.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.600
Even though the dollar gain was modest.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.240
The earnings catalyst was real enough to keep the name green,

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:51.420
but a single name FinTech sleeve can still lag a broader tech rotation on a day

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.880
when the tape is picking winners by sector, not by press release.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.340
That is the film session takeaway.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.520
A beat and a raise are not a sector tailwind,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:06.160
and sizing as if they were is how a good fundamental call becomes a mediocre relative result.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:12.060
Rule. Always cross-check sector rotation signals before sizing earnings driven entries.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:15.920
If you want those probabilities on a screen instead of in your head,

